<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/fluid-mechanics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fluid mechanics for engineers is the <strong>physics lesson made measurable and
    money-relevant</strong>: pipes sized by pressure drop, pumps chosen from performance curves,
    and Reynolds number deciding what regime every flow is in. Nearly every engineered system —
    water, air, fuel, blood-loop devices — is a fluid circuit.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="boundary layers, Reynolds, pipe economics, drag" widget="equationStep" config={fluidEngCfg} />

  <h2>Applied chapters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pumps and system curves:</strong> the pump has a curve (head vs flow), the system has one (static + friction) — the operating point is the intersection. Cavitation (vapour bubbles collapsing) destroys impellers when suction pressure drops too low — the NPSH calculation is every pump engineer\'s reflex.</li>
    <li><strong>Open-channel flow:</strong> rivers, canals, sewers — hydraulic jumps (the standing wave below weirs) dissipate energy deliberately in spillways stilling basins (the geography lesson\'s rivers, engineered).</li>
    <li><strong>Aerodynamics feeds everything:</strong> car drag coefficients fell from ~0.5 (1970s boxes) to ~0.22 (best sedans) — worth ~15% fuel; the CFD tools that got there now design every building\'s wind environment too.</li>
    <li><strong>Biomedical fluids:</strong> blood is non-Newtonian, arteries are elastic, flow is pulsatile — artificial hearts and stents are designed by the same equations with harder constraints (the medicine ladder\'s circulation lesson, engineered).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> White, Fluid Mechanics; Munson et al.; the physics ladder\'s fluid-dynamics lesson (Bernoulli, Navier–Stokes frontier).</p>
</TopicPage>
